Hi

Unsure about how the DEC architecture works but in i386 systems the card in
the first PCI slot takes precedence over the second third etc.

Try swapping the cards around.

Regards

-----Original Message-----
From: Andrew Maier [mailto:andrew@maier.name]
Sent: Wednesday, 5 November 2003 9:49 AM
To: debian-alpha@lists.debian.org
Subject: boot time order of scsi disks


Hi everybody,

assumming one has two scsi cards, is there a way of specifying in which
order the cards are recoginzed during boot time?

The problem I just hooked up a disk to my (up to now) unused Adaptec
7871 card. My boot disk are connected to the built-in qlogic card of my
PWS-500au. Much to my horror I have disovered that the adaptec cards is
recogised first, thus the disk connected becomes /dev/sda, wheres my
internal boot disk (which was /dev/sda) becomes /dev/sdb.

Since the new disk is external, I may or may not decide to power them
up (don't ask, its essentially a noise problem), so I don't want to go
through the process of having to rename /etc/fstab, etc.

What are my options? (BTW currently both scsi drivers are compiled into
the kernel).

Thanks in advance,

	Andrew

