I'm okay with this sugesstion, as long as Debian on Alpha enables
fast math by exception and not as the rule.  My concern is also
dependencies - people will insist on building glibc, etc with fast
math enabled (because by default they'll work fine) and then some other
application with throw down IEEE values to them an they'll crash.
The application (which functions perfectly on every other=20
architecture in the Debian world) would then be blamed (even though
is was built IEEE compliant) and we are back at having random
applications crash.

BTW, I debugged the kde library that was causing my problem.
Sure enough there is a comment in the code that they'll need to
deal with -0, NaN and Inf.  It's not done yet though - I wonder
why?  perhaps because everything worked anyway on the platform
that the author was testing.

...tom

